WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.685
Step 2: open coding.

2
00:00:01.965 --> 00:00:05.379
For every failing trace, write a short note about what went wrong.

3
00:00:05.659 --> 00:00:12.976
This is open coding, a term the FAQ borrows from qualitative research, where it compares the activity to journaling.

4
00:00:13.256 --> 00:00:16.648
The rules are few and they matter: Don’t start with categories.

5
00:00:16.928 --> 00:00:21.771
Write what you see in plain words: “Says QuickBooks Desktop sync exists; it doesn’t.

6
00:00:22.051 --> 00:00:33.251
” Husain’s field guide contrasts this bottom-up approach with top-down lists like “hallucination, toxicity, verbosity,” which tend to miss the failures specific to your product.

7
00:00:33.531 --> 00:00:35.280
Note the first thing that went wrong.

8
00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:42.728
In a multi-step trace, one early mistake (bad retrieval, a misread question) causes the visible ones downstream.

9
00:00:43.008 --> 00:00:45.525
Fixing the first error often fixes the rest.

10
00:00:45.805 --> 00:00:47.533
Be specific enough to act on.

11
00:00:47.813 --> 00:00:49.392
“Bad answer” is useless.

12
00:00:49.672 --> 00:00:54.344
“Answered about multi-currency when asked about per-line tax” points at a fix.

13
00:00:54.624 --> 00:00:56.779
Note good behavior that surprised you.

14
00:00:57.059 --> 00:01:03.821
A bot that correctly says “I can’t find that, let me connect you with the team” is worth protecting with a regression test.

15
00:01:04.101 --> 00:01:06.128
This is slow at first and gets fast.

16
00:01:06.408 --> 00:01:11.272
It is also where a domain expert earns their keep, because “is this answer correct?

17
00:01:11.552 --> 00:01:14.624
” often needs product knowledge an engineer doesn’t have.
